---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UuidGenerator from '@/components/tools/dev/UuidGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="uuid-generator">
  <UuidGenerator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate UUIDs</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick <strong>v4</strong> for pure randomness or <strong>v7</strong> for identifiers that sort by creation time.</li>
      <li>Set how many you need, up to 1000 at a time.</li>
      <li>Choose the formatting: hyphens on or off, upper or lower case, braces for a Microsoft-style GUID.</li>
      <li>Pick a layout (one per line, comma separated, or a JSON array), then copy or download the batch.</li>
      <li>Use the <strong>Validate</strong> tab to check an existing identifier and read its version, variant and embedded timestamp.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The anatomy of a UUID</h2>
    <p>
      A UUID is 128 bits, written as 32 hexadecimal digits in five groups: <code>8-4-4-4-12</code>. Two
      positions are reserved. The first digit of the third group is the version, and the first digit of the
      fourth group encodes the variant. The other bits depend on the version.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Version</th><th>How it is built</th><th>Sortable</th><th>Good for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>v1</td><td>100-nanosecond timestamp plus the machine MAC address</td><td>Poorly: the timestamp bytes are in the wrong order</td><td>Legacy systems</td></tr>
        <tr><td>v3 / v5</td><td>MD5 / SHA-1 of a namespace plus a name</td><td>No</td><td>Deterministic ids derived from a stable name</td></tr>
        <tr><td>v4</td><td>122 random bits</td><td>No</td><td>The general-purpose default</td></tr>
        <tr><td>v6</td><td>v1 with the timestamp bytes reordered</td><td>Yes</td><td>Migrating away from v1 while keeping ordering</td></tr>
        <tr><td>v7</td><td>48-bit Unix millisecond timestamp, then randomness</td><td>Yes</td><td>Database primary keys</td></tr>
        <tr><td>v8</td><td>Whatever you define, within the version and variant bits</td><td>Depends</td><td>Custom schemes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>v4 or v7 for a database primary key?</h2>
    <p>
      This is the important choice. A v4 UUID is uniformly random, so consecutive inserts go to random positions
      in a B-tree index. Pages split all the time, the working set no longer fits in memory, and write
      throughput falls as the table grows. The effect is worst in storage engines that cluster rows by primary
      key, such as InnoDB.
    </p>
    <p>
      A v7 UUID puts the millisecond timestamp in the high bits, so new rows go to the right-hand edge of the
      index, as with an auto-increment integer. It keeps the UUID properties: generated client-side, globally
      unique, with no coordination between services.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost is disclosure. A v7 identifier tells anyone who sees it when the record was created, to the
      millisecond, and lets them order two records in time. For an invoice id that is fine. For a
      password-reset token or an unlisted share link, use v4, or better a dedicated random token, because a
      UUID's dashes and version bits waste six characters of entropy.
    </p>

    <h2>How likely is a collision?</h2>
    <p>
      A v4 UUID has 122 random bits. By the birthday bound you must generate about 2.7 × 10<sup>18</sup> of them
      for a 50% chance of one duplicate: about 85 years at a billion per second. In practice the real risk is a
      broken random source, such as a virtual machine cloned with a fixed seed or a library that falls back to
      <code>Math.random</code>. This page always uses <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Within one millisecond, the v7 generator increments a 12-bit counter in place of new random middle bits,
      so a batch of 1000 generated in the same instant is still strictly ordered.
    </p>

    <h2>UUID formats</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Where</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Canonical</td><td><code>0195c1f4-1b2c-7a3d-9e4f-5a6b7c8d9e0f</code></td><td>RFC 9562, JSON, most APIs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Braced</td><td><code>&#123;0195C1F4-1B2C-7A3D-9E4F-5A6B7C8D9E0F&#125;</code></td><td>Windows registry, COM, .NET <code>Guid</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Compact</td><td><code>0195c1f41b2c7a3d9e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f</code></td><td>URLs, file names, cache keys</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nil</td><td><code>00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000</code></td><td>A defined "no value" placeholder</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Always compare UUIDs case-insensitively: the standard says generators must write lower case and parsers
      must accept both, and much Microsoft tooling writes upper case.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
